0a48187a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131460" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131460/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131654/selection/6273a1ce826479f6324c67b6111789f3a2ae7f61836a9bf7e56848eb8a1214e1" :answer/id #uuid "38e94d08-1452-54f2-8eb4-b30799269ce7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :